Two findings by karr-pod-writer while documenting Role/BoardAccess.pm for
#118. Neither was introduced there; both were verified against the code.

1. delete_task logs even when nothing was deleted.

    sub save_task {   ... return $wrote unless $wrote;
                      $self->log_task_write(...); return $wrote; }
    sub delete_task { my $result = $self->store->delete_task($id);
                      $self->log_task_write($id); return $result; }

save_task guards the activity-log write on a successful store write;
delete_task calls log_task_write unconditionally, so deleting an id that does
not exist still appends an entry to refs/karr/log/*. Since #64 that log feeds
`karr log` and `karr show --me`, so this shows up as an action that never
happened. Decide whether the guard belongs on delete_task too, or whether a
delete attempt is deliberately worth logging either way -- but the two should
not disagree by accident.

2. Role::BoardAccess::save_config is unreachable, and would misbehave.

    sub save_config { my ($self, $effective) = @_;
                      $effective //= $self->config;
                      return $self->store->save_config($effective); }

No caller anywhere in lib/ or t/ invokes $self->save_config; every config write
goes through $self->store->save_config($hash) directly (Cmd::Config, Cmd::Init).
The zero-argument fallback hands BoardStore::save_config the `config` attribute
from Role::BoardDiscovery, which is an App::karr::Config OBJECT, whereas
BoardStore::save_config expects a plain effective-config hash -- it reads
$effective->{version} and passes it to _diff_hashes against the defaults. The
keys do not line up.

So the fallback is both unused and wrong. Either delete the role method (the
honest option if nothing wants it), or fix the fallback to hand over the hash
and give it a caller. It is documented as-is for now, explicitly saying no
command uses the no-argument form.
Both findings fixed in lib/App/karr/Role/BoardAccess.pm; regression test t/133-boardaccess-write-doors.t.

1) delete_task now guards its log write on the store result (return $result unless $result), matching save_task. Rationale: the log answers what happened to the board; BoardStore::delete_task returns false when nothing was removed (id absent, or delete failed), and an entry has no room to say 'attempted' -- agent/action/task_id only -- so an attempt entry is indistinguishable from a real delete. TaskMutation::delete_task_guarded already dies on a missing id before logging, so all three write paths now agree.

2) Role save_config removed (sub + POD), replaced by a =head2 'Writing the config' note. It was unreachable (no caller in lib/, t/, bin/) and worse than the ticket said: the no-arg form does NOT die. Verified empirically -- passing the App::karr::Config object writes a refs/karr/config with the blessed hash's 'data:' and 'file: ~' at top level, burying the real config and losing board.name, and it passes BoardStore's validation gate. One-arg form was a bare pass-through adding nothing, so fixing the fallback would have meant inventing a caller.

